== K1 survival product (Thm 2.2(a)) ==
  (1/2;1/2)_inf = 0.288788095086602421278899721929
  direct product  = 0.288788095086602421278899721929
  record's value 0.288788...: agrees to 6 digits: True
  partial products K=1..5: ['1/2', '3/8', '21/64', '315/1024', '9765/32768']
  equal to c1's list [1/2, 3/8, 21/64, 315/1024, 9765/32768]: True
== K2 Sigma_1 facts for K_A on the lottery stream (Cor 2.6') ==
  K=1: facts = 3 (= 2^(K+1) + K - 2: True), bits for the count = 2 <= K+2 = 3
  K=2: facts = 8 (= 2^(K+1) + K - 2: True), bits for the count = 4 <= K+2 = 4
  K=3: facts = 17 (= 2^(K+1) + K - 2: True), bits for the count = 5 <= K+2 = 5
  K=10: facts = 2056 (= 2^(K+1) + K - 2: True), bits for the count = 12 <= K+2 = 12
  K=60: facts = 2305843009213694010 (= 2^(K+1) + K - 2: True), bits for the count = 62 <= K+2 = 62
  K=1..60: facts < 2^(K+1)+K: True; count fits in K+2 bits: True
  horizon m(n) = sum_(k<ceil(n/2)) (k+1) <= (n+1)^2 for n < 2000: True
  batch lottery: max over n < 5000 of horizon/(n+1)^2 = 0.2500 (<= 1: True)
== K3 colouring constants (Prop 3.8) ==
  d=2..10^6: pigeonhole 3^(2^l-1) < 2^d fails in 0 cases; l > log2 d - 2 fails in 0
  (largest pigeonhole l) - (record's l): distribution {0: 999966, 1: 33}
  exact integer pigeonhole test for d < 200: failures 0
== K4 length-lexicographic codes (Cor 3.6) ==
  m=0..12: largest code of a string of length <= m equals P_m = 2^(m+1)-2: True
  h_m <= 2^m needs m+1 bits (0..2^m has 2^m + 1 values): True
== K5 delta (Thm 6.1) ==
  delta_str = 1/(5 log2 14) = 0.052530
  n = 1e6: n^delta_str = 2.066
  n = 1e9: n^delta_str = 2.970
  n = 1e30: n^delta_str = 37.661
  n = 1e100: n^delta_str = 179056.751
== K6 binomial bound (Cor 6.2) ==
  log2 C(n,u) <= u log2(e n/u) for 1 <= u <= n <= 400: violations 0
done
